meta {
  name: Set Audit Assignment
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/deal-audits/deals/{{auditDealId}}/assignment
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "assignee": { "type": "user", "id": "{{currentUserId}}" }
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns the audit with both owners resolved", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("auditStatus");
    expect(body).to.have.property("assignee");
    expect(body.assignee).to.have.property("type");
    expect(body.assignee).to.have.property("id");
    expect(body.assignee).to.have.property("name");
  });
}

docs {
  # PATCH /deal-audits/deals/:dealId/assignment — set assignee / reviewer (PAC-72)

  Sets who is responsible for a deal's audit. **Ownership is per deal, not per
  item**: the reviewer works item by item, but "who is on this" and the final
  verdict belong to the audit as a whole.

  - **Module:** `deal_audits`
  - **Permission:** `deal_audits:write` (`write ⇒ read`)
  - **Data scope:** the caller must be able to reach the audit — see the note on
    reachability below.

  ## The two roles
  | slot     | does                                                            |
  |----------|-----------------------------------------------------------------|
  | assignee | gathers the evidence, uploads documents, submits for review     |
  | reviewer | approves, requests changes, or sends it back                    |

  Each slot holds a **user or a role**. A role assignee is a queue — "whoever on
  the CRM team picks it up".

  > 🔴 `id` is **always an ObjectId**, including for a role: store the role's
  > `_id`, never its slug. A field that is sometimes an id and sometimes a name
  > cannot be indexed, forces every read to branch, and breaks the moment
  > someone renames a role. Display names are resolved on read.

  ## Path params
  | param  | type   | notes                                         |
  |--------|--------|-----------------------------------------------|
  | dealId | string | the **Deal** `_id` — not the audit's own id   |

  ## Body — at least one key required
  | field    | type          | notes                                        |
  |----------|---------------|----------------------------------------------|
  | assignee | object\|null  | `{ type: "user"\|"role", id }`; `null` clears |
  | reviewer | object\|null  | same shape                                    |

  Omitting a key leaves that slot untouched; sending `null` **clears** it. The
  two are distinct: an audit whose reviewer left the agency has to be
  un-assignable, not merely re-assignable.

  ```json
  { "assignee": { "type": "role", "id": "665f...ab2" }, "reviewer": null }
  ```

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  {
    "id": "665f...9c1",
    "dealId": "665f...a03",
    "auditStatus": "Not Submitted",
    "assignee": { "type": "role", "id": "665f...ab2", "name": "CRM" },
    "reviewer": null,
    "submittedAt": null,
    "reviewedAt": null,
    "reasonCodes": [],
    "auditScore": 0,
    "auditNotes": null,
    "itemCount": 7,
    "resolvedCount": 2,
    "openCount": 5,
    "completionPct": 29
  }
  ```

  ## Reachability — who can load an audit at all
  Access to a specific audit is **assignee OR reviewer** (matched against the
  caller's user id *and* their role ids). This is deliberately a different
  question from *whose board it appears on*, which is scoped to the **assignee**
  only — using the board's clamp here would `404` the very reviewer the review
  endpoint exists for. Agency- and branch-scoped callers reach it through their
  own scope as usual.

  ⚠ Reassigning an audit **removes it from the previous assignee's board**. For
  an `own`-scoped producer that means losing sight of a deal they sold.

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — empty body, or an `id` that is not a 24-char hex id.
  - `403 Forbidden` — module disabled or missing `deal_audits:write`.
  - `404 Not Found` — no audit for that deal, or it is out of the caller's reach.

  ## Side effects
  Appends an `audit_assigned` entry to the audit's thread (see **List Audit
  Notes**). Best-effort: a failed timeline write is logged, not surfaced.
}
